---
import Case from '../../layouts/Case.astro';
import CaseSection from '../../components/case/CaseSection.astro';
import WorkVisual from '../../components/WorkVisual.astro';
---

<Case
  slug="the-signal"
  title="The Signal"
  headline="Fewer alerts,"
  headlineAccent="better care."
  summary="Nurses face an avalanche of EHR alerts — most irrelevant, many ignored. The Signal is a concept for a decision-support dashboard that surfaces only the patient changes that need action now."
  description="The Signal: a concept by Jonathan Lerma for a clinical decision-support dashboard that reduces EHR alert fatigue."
  meta={[
    { label: 'Type', value: 'Self-initiated concept' },
    { label: 'Role', value: 'Product designer' },
    { label: 'Domain', value: 'Healthcare · clinical decision support' },
    { label: 'Focus', value: 'Data visualization, triage, information density' },
  ]}
  links={[{ label: 'Open the prototype', href: 'https://the-signal.jonathanlerma.com' }]}
  accent="var(--pance-color-violet-400)"
>
  <CaseSection id="note" number="00" eyebrow="A note" title="This is" accent="a concept.">
    <p>
      The Signal hasn’t been used in a hospital. It’s an exploration of a well-documented problem, and the numbers below
      are the outcomes it would need to prove — not results.
    </p>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection id="problem" number="01" eyebrow="Problem" title="When everything is urgent," accent="nothing is.">
    <p>
      Electronic health records raise alerts for almost every change. Most are low value, so nurses learn to dismiss
      them — and the critical one gets lost in the noise. That is alert fatigue, and it feeds burnout.
    </p>
    <p class="quote">
      The design question: how do you show <em>less</em> without ever hiding what matters?
    </p>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection id="concept" number="02" eyebrow="Concept" title="Three levels" accent="of attention.">
    <div class="cards">
      <div>
        <p class="mono">Act now</p>
        <h3>Trends, not thresholds</h3>
        <p>A falling blood pressure across three readings outranks one odd value.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="mono">Watch</p>
        <h3>Visible, not loud</h3>
        <p>Changes worth knowing stay on the board without interrupting.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="mono">FYI</p>
        <h3>Batched</h3>
        <p>Routine results arrive together, at natural breaks in the shift.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="vignette">
      <WorkVisual kind="signal" />
    </div>
    <p class="mono">Rebuilt with Pance components. Patient data is invented.</p>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection id="measure" number="03" eyebrow="What it would need to prove" title="Hypotheses," accent="not results.">
    <ul class="ticks">
      <li>
        <strong>Fewer interruptions:</strong> alerts per nurse per shift fall substantially, with no missed critical
        events.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Faster response</strong> to the alerts that remain.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Trust:</strong> nurses rate the board as reliable enough to stop double-checking the EHR.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>The next step would be a study with a clinical team — the part a concept can’t skip.</p>
  </CaseSection>
</Case>
